    "result_text": "Green. Build succeeds and the acceptance test passes with the refactored wiring.\n\n## What I changed in response to the review\n\nI **agreed with the finding** \u2014 the count was being computed twice (two full `findAll()` scans of the same data in one POST pipeline), and my \"cannot drift\" comment overstated what a shared static *method* bought (the two steps still recomputed independently, even against subtly different inputs \u2014 `request.getCity()` vs. `owner.getCity()`).\n\nFixes:\n\n1. **New `CityOwnerCount` qualifier** \u2014 a custom `@Qualifier` annotation (OfficeFloor's documented, type-safe form, preferred over the stringly-typed `@Qualified`). I verified the POST pipeline has no other `Integer` variable, so there's no collision, but qualifying makes the intent explicit and refactor-safe as the review suggested.\n2. **`CheckOwnerCityCapacity`** now publishes the count it already computes as `@CityOwnerCount Out<Integer>` and still throws at `>= MAX`. I inlined the counting loop back (the shared static helper is no longer needed) and dropped the cross-class static call.\n3. **`CheckOwnerCityCapacityWarning`** now reads `@CityOwnerCount @Val Integer` instead of re-scanning via `OwnerRepository`. It no longer injects the repository at all.\n4. **Javadoc on both classes** rewritten so the \"cannot drift\" claim is now accurate \u2014 it's one scan, one count, handed between steps (and I note the count is identical at both points because the owner is still unsaved when either runs).\n\nThe persistence/DTO/OpenAPI plumbing and the create-time semantics are unchanged, so all acceptance behaviour is intact.",
