        "findings": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe new field, DTO/schema/OpenAPI plumbing, and the dedicated warning step class are all placed correctly and read naturally for this codebase. The one structural issue is in how the city count is shared between the two steps.\n\n**Finding \u2014 the owner count is recomputed with a second full-table scan instead of passed as a variable**\n\n`CheckOwnerCityCapacity.service` already computes `countOwnersInCity(...)` (a full `ownerRepository.findAll()` scan) and then throws it away. `CheckOwnerCityCapacityWarning.service` runs later in the *same* POST pipeline (steps `checkCityCapacity` \u2192 ... \u2192 `flagCityCapacityWarning`) and scans `findAll()` all over again for the same city. The counting work is duplicated.\n\nThis codebase has a first-class idiom for exactly this \u2014 `Out<T>` / `@Val` to hand a computed value from one step to the next (AGENTS.md: \"Steps do not call each other\u2026 publish state into a variable\"). The natural shape here is:\n\n- `CheckOwnerCityCapacity` declares `Out<Integer>` (qualified, e.g. `@CityOwnerCount`), sets the count it already computed, and still throws at `>= MAX`.\n- `CheckOwnerCityCapacityWarning` reads `@Val Integer` and sets `capacityWarning = count >= WARN && count < MAX`.\n\nThat removes the second scan, drops the cross-class `static` call between two step classes (which cuts against the \"steps are decoupled\" grain), and makes the drift claim actually true.\n\nNote the Javadoc on both classes oversells the current coupling: sharing the `countOwnersInCity` *method* does not make the warning and rejection \"cannot drift\" \u2014 they recompute independently, and in fact against different inputs (`request.getCity()` vs. the built `owner.getCity()`). Passing the single computed value is what would make the \"cannot drift\" statement correct; if you keep the recompute approach, soften that comment instead of claiming a coupling that isn't there."
      }
